The test runner exists both in GraalJS (testRunner.js) and Node (node_testRunner.js). The loadModule wraps code in `(function(module, exports, require) {...})` — so a module file can use `module.exports`. For the browser, the same file needs to attach to `window`. Dual pattern:

```js
(function (root, factory) {
  var api = factory();
  if (typeof module !== 'undefined' && module.exports) module.exports = api;
  else root.FACTORY_STEPS = api;
})(typeof window !== 'undefined' ? window : globalThis, function () { ... });
```

Wait — in loadModule eval scope, `module` is the param. In browser IIFE, `module` is undefined. `typeof module !== 'undefined'` works in both. And GraalJS testRunner? Let me check testRunner.js loadModule to ensure the same signature. Let me check quickly.

Now check whether there's a run_all.json listing test files — my new test needs registration? Check run_all.json and whether the repo's CI runs all tests. Also, per TDD rules, tests go in `js/unit-tests/`. The board code is in site/factory though. I'll create `site/factory/steps.js` (pure logic: step computation) + test `js/unit-tests/test_factoryBoardSteps.js` + run json. Hmm, but wait — maybe better to keep board JS self-contained (zero build, "Copy this folder" deploys). Adding a second JS file breaks the "copy folder, edit config" story slightly but README says config.js + app.js + styles.css; a steps.js is fine to copy along. Alternatively keep everything in app.js and have the test load app.js with heavy DOM mocks... app.js immediately runs the IIFE and touches document. Mocks would need getElementById returning stub elements, fetch, setInterval, etc. Possible (mock `document`, `window`, `location`, `fetch`, `setInterval`, localStorage, matchMedia...). That's a big mock surface.

Cleaner: extract pure logic into `site/factory/steps.js`:
- `stepsFor(card, laneId)` → array of {id, name, state: 'done'|'current'|'pending'|'failed', at, title}
- app.js uses it to render the stepper HTML.
Tests target steps.js. This follows SRP and is testable in the GraalJS/Node harness.

Also the lane-state mapping + failed verdict detection live there. app.js keeps rendering (HTML + tooltips).

